<section>
    <p class="eyebrow">Step 2</p>
    <h3>Add the attribute to the test</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php" data-trim data-line-numbers="1-15|6|9-12">
use Symfony\Bridge\PhpUnit\Attribute\UseRecord;
use Symfony\Bundle\FrameworkBundle\Test\WebTestCase;

class GetRandomControllerTest extends WebTestCase
{
    #[UseRecord]
    public function testRandom(): void
    {
        $client = static::createClient();
        $client->request('GET', '/random');

        $this->assertResponseIsSuccessful();
    }
}
    </code></pre>
    <p class="fragment subtitle" style="font-size: 0.6em;">The test uses <code>GetRandomControllerTest/testRandom.har</code>, stored next to it. Nothing else changes in the code.</p>
    <aside class="notes">
        No configuration step: when framework.test is true, the recorder is enabled by default. It decorates http_client.transport, as close to the network as possible, so every client, default and scoped, is recorded in a single file per test. The attribute only names the file. Recording is decided when you launch the tests, with the environment variable: nothing that touches the network can be committed by mistake. The test code does not change: the controller calls an external API, and that call is what gets recorded and then replayed. Outside PHPUnit, you build the client by hand with a RecorderConfiguration; two extension points: RecorderConfigurationInterface and RedactorInterface.
    </aside>
</section>
